## 2. Theoretical Framework

### 2.1 Classical Binary Logic and Its Limits

Classical binary logic operates on the principle of bivalence: every proposition has exactly one truth value. This is computationally efficient and formally consistent. However, it is *incomplete* in the Gödelian sense (Gödel, 1931). The system cannot account for its own meta-position. The excluded middle, as a principle, exists *outside* the set of propositions it governs, rendering it both foundational and excluded.

### 2.2 Paraconsistency and the Tolerance of Contradiction

Paraconsistent logic challenges the classical assumption that contradictions entail explosion (Priest, 2006). In paraconsistent systems, \( P \land \neg P \) does not imply any arbitrary \( Q \). This opens a space where the excluded middle is not a prohibition but a *region* of coexistence. We propose that the excluded middle is the *ground* of paraconsistency—not a third truth value, but the *condition* for truth-value assignment.

### 2.3 Fuzzy Logic and Degrees of Truth

Fuzzy logic posits that truth is a matter of degree, ranging from 0 to 1 (Zadeh, 1965). This continuum is not a third value but an *infinite* middle. We argue that this continuum is a formal approximation of the excluded middle as a *space of becoming*, not a fixed position.

### 2.4 Dialetheism and True Contradictions

Dialetheism (Priest, 2006) holds that some contradictions are true. If the excluded middle is the boundary between truth and falsity, then the boundary itself is a true contradiction—simultaneously included and excluded. This is not a failure of logic but a feature of its application to dynamic, relational systems.

